WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.548
(pulsating music)

00:00:03.548 --> 00:00:06.700
(slow orchestral music)

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.190
- [Brian] On January 5th, 2012,

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.970
a group calling itself 3301

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.990
posted a mysterious
message on the internet.

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:15.920
- [Computer] Hello.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.600
We are looking for highly
intelligent individuals.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.870
To find them, we have devised a test.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.590
There is a message hidden in this image.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.170
- [Brian] The hidden
message led to a series

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.540
of other riddles, codes
leading to phone messages,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.450
which provided GPS coordinates
for posters around the world.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.700
- A mysterious group has been posting

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.370
extremely complicated
puzzles on the internet.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.820
- [Reporter] Solving the puzzles requires

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.060
an esoteric knowledge,
computer programming,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.840
hidden codes, history, art, and literature

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.160
as obscure as Medieval Welsh poetry.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.060
- [Brian] The puzzle was called Cicada

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.200
and appeared to be a recruiting tool

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.023
for a secretive organization
that called itself 3301.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.720
- [Marcus] I was obsessed.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.403
It was just all Cicada, all the time.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.250
I didn't sleep a whole lot.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.393
I was basically just holed up in my room.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.020
I really wanted to find the answer.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.620
- [Brian] In January 2013,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.500
a new Cicada puzzle appeared
with more elaborate clues,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.843
surrounding Cicada with
even more intrigue.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.550
- There was not a class that went by

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.770
and not a shift of work that
I didn't have my laptop open.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.160
It was my life.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.980
- [Brian] No one knew
who created the puzzles

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.010
or what their intentions were.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.340
- [Marcus] I wasn't sure if it was like

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.510
a government recruitment or
some underground hacking group.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.840
I'm torn then and still torn.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.650
- [Brian] And soon, Cicada became

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.810
one of the greatest
mysteries of the internet.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.180
- This is hugely elaborate.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.446
It's a very, very involved game.

00:01:46.446 --> 00:01:49.908
(orchestral music)

00:01:49.908 --> 00:01:52.070
- [Brian] In 2014, the
puzzle reached its height

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.110
of complexity, with a
book of encrypted symbols

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.960
called the "Liber Primus."

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.510
It's 58 pages long.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.330
- Two of them are solved to this day.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.000
The contents of the rest
are a complete mystery.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.820
- The "Liber Primus" it's encrypted,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.250
which obscures the
original message, right?

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:13.750
And the search space itself
for the possible messages

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.610
that it could be is nearly infinity.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.450
- [Brian] The "Liber Primus" was so hard,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.070
most people just gave up.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.090
But a small group of
amateur puzzle solvers

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.600
from around the world banded
together and refused to quit.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.840
- [Onecool] There's no one person

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.870
who's gonna be better than a giant group

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.470
of like-minded individuals
working together.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.650
- We have never had a puzzle that we took

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.860
that much time to solve
and still could not do it.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.630
- [Shadow Walker] We
can get into these zones

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.970
where we focus on one thing so intensely,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.390
we become consumed by it.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.390
Other people might
consider it an obsession,

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.470
but to us this is perfectly normal.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.160
- If I were to explain
Cicada in just one sentence,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.740
it's a puzzle after a
puzzle after a puzzle,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.410
presumably leading to some payoff.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.670
Even when you get to the payoff,

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.140
you are left wondering what
exactly just happened to you.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.390
- [Computer] The key has always been

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.360
right in front of your eyes.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.457
Good luck, 3301.

00:03:07.457 --> 00:03:10.040
(gentle music)

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.680
- [Brian] I'm Brian Bura.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.623
I've been an investigative
journalist for 35 years.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.383
I've taken on mysteries large
and small all over the world.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.933
New York, Hollywood, Tokyo,
Jerusalem, Kathmandu.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.330
There are very few major
mysteries in the world

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.890
that ever stay mysteries forever.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.970
Cicada, it's out there,
and it's an unknown.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.580
What is this thing?

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.190
Why is this thing?

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.263
Who is this thing?

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.900
These individuals from all over the planet

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.613
have been working on
Cicada puzzles since 2012.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.560
I am dying to know what makes them tick,

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.440
what drives them on this
almost Sisyphean task

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.843
that is Cicada 3301.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.470
They've spent three years

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.230
trying to find an answer
to the "Libra Primus,"

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.089
and members of the group
as still searching.

00:04:10.089 --> 00:04:11.388
- [Woman] You think it's unsolvable?

00:04:11.388 --> 00:04:13.079
- We used to think it was unsolvable,

00:04:13.079 --> 00:04:15.910
but like now we think it could be.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.400
- [Man] Do you have a
printout, let's look at it.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.314
- I'm not gonna magically
solve it by looking at it.

00:04:20.314 --> 00:04:21.957
- No, no, like it'll--
- There's some cool

00:04:21.957 --> 00:04:23.090
art in there.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.562
It's been our life.

00:04:24.562 --> 00:04:26.024
- Sounds obsessive.

00:04:26.024 --> 00:04:28.750
- [Man] It is obsessive.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.010
- [Brian] They are convinced
a solution is out there.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.570
They're still hopeful, because one of them

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.250
actually solved the
first puzzle back in 2012

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.640
and got further into the world of Cicada

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.850
than anyone before or since.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.760
And he was only 15 years old at the time.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.810
- I'm Marcus Wanner, and
I'm one of the people

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.333
who solved the original
Cicada puzzle in 2012.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.120
I was just about your
average 15-year-old kid.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.330
I grew up in rural Southwest Virginia.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.460
I didn't feel like I really
had a whole lot of friends

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.390
because I was homeschooled.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.220
Yeah, I missed out on a few things,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.100
but most of it came down to bull crap

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.883
high school relationships and
bull crap middle school drama.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.240
I had a lot of free time,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.230
playing around and exploring stuff.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:18.810
And I was always really curious,

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.640
but what I was actually doing

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.213
was building a career by accident.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.840
My dad was an electrical engineer,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.293
and he showed me how to solder and stuff.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.130
I spent about two or three years

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.320
frying every single electronic
component that I touched,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.025
because I didn't understand what they did.

00:05:37.025 --> 00:05:39.570
I used to make fireworks,
little bombs and stuff.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.930
We made a mortar that shot
tennis balls around one time.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.060
You take a bunch of Pringles
tubes and duct tape,

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.390
fill it up with a little gasoline,

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:47.470
and you light the gas fumes,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.430
and suddenly your tennis ball
is in the neighbor's yard.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.250
I like to use technology to
solve interesting problems

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.933
and do things that you wouldn't
normally be able to do.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.420
I was just hanging out on
4chan one Saturday night,

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.850
'cause that's what homeschool kids do.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.450
There's a lot of really
disturbing content on there.

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.410
It's definitely not a place

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.680
where you take everything
that's said at face value,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.213
because nobody has a
name attached to them.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.100
I was on the science and math board.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.170
I saw the 3301 image recruiting people.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.977
And I'm like, "Well, how would
I hide data in this image?"

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.560
There was an IRC server link in the thread

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:36.690
that was on the board.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.623
It's an online chatroom, right, IRC.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.120
We had two or three messages a second

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.150
that were popping up in this chatroom.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.890
It's scrolling faster
than you can read it.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.673
I don't think I slept that night.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.110
What they had done is
just taken plain text

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.550
and stuck it on the end of the files.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.500
It was pretty easy to
tell that it was a URL,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.310
because it was one letter,
two of the same letter,

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.190
two different letters, and
then two of the same thing,

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:05.180
which is, you know, http://.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.053
You get the idea.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.270
We got to the URL, and
it was actually a duck.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.900
We were supposed to take these words.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.630
We'll see if you can guess
how to get the message out.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:17.387
They recognized out and
guess and were like,

00:07:17.387 --> 00:07:19.180
"Oh, OutGuess."

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.510
OutGuess is just a tool that you can use

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.963
to hide text inside of a JPEG image.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.270
They're meant to be impossible to detect.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.760
That's the idea steganography

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.710
or hiding messages
inside of other messages.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.670
The stego stuff is more
like if you went into a room

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:39.870
with a china cabinet and
you rearranged the china

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.040
in the cabinet slightly, like blue cup

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.493
is on the third shelf,
to signal a message.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:49.340
Stego tools like OutGuess,
it's like just trying

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.900
a bunch of different ways of
interpreting the china cabinet

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.100
till you find one where
a message falls out.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.330
After we did the OutGuess,

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.230
it started giving us a phone number.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.351
We were like, "Oh, a phone
number, that's weird."

00:08:01.351 --> 00:08:04.500
(phone dial beeping)

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.700
We had no idea what to
expect at that point.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.370
- [Computer] Very good,
you have done well.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:11.460
There are three prime numbers associated

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.870
with the original final .JPEG image.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:17.060
3301 is one of them.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.510
You will have to find the other two.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:21.230
- It was just a generic phone number.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.480
We tried to pull the records on it.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:25.170
Some people tried to social engineer

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.440
some folks who worked there.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.020
And it turns out that their
customer support people

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:30.560
couldn't actually access the records

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.290
for that particular number.

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:33.820
I thought that was kind of weird.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:35.920
- [Computer] Multiply all
three of these numbers together

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.130
and add a .com on the end
to find the next step.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.890
Good luck, goodbye.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:43.410
- So Cicada told us that
there were three prime numbers

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.510
in the original message
that we had started with.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.670
First one was 3301, but the other two

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:48.690
were a little bit harder to find.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.310
They were just the
dimensions of the image.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.513
So we multiplied them and
we got this .com address.

00:08:54.788 --> 00:08:57.480
It was just a plain white
website with a cicada on it

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.890
and a countdown timer to like
a day or two in the future.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:01.960
Once the countdown was over,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.100
they posted all of these coordinates

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.730
in different places across the world.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.373
Moscow, Sydney, Seoul.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:12.588
Got it right there.

00:09:12.588 --> 00:09:15.090
It's taped, you can see the corners.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:18.170
Suddenly, it's not, we just
need to solve this puzzle.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.130
It looks the same on everybody's screens.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.130
Who gives a shit where we are?

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:23.380
We started reaching out to people

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.460
and actually figuring
out where everybody was.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.410
And that was how we found out

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:28.830
some of us lived near each other

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:30.500
and sort of laid the groundwork

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.373
for the community that's
sprung up ever since.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.067
So the QR code led us to "Agrippa."

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:42.170
A science fiction author
named William Gibson

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:45.510
created this "Agrippa" poem
that was both printed in a book

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.500
where the pages would
dissolve when exposed to air,

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.350
and also on a floppy drive
that would erase itself

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.800
after printing out the
text of the book once.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:55.490
It just disappears.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.340
But we were able to get a copy of it.

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:00.930
A few weeks later, we got a signed

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.550
and encrypted email from 3301.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.820
- [Computer] We are not a hacker group.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:09.820
- [Marcus] Cicada said
overtly that they weren't into

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.460
doing any illegal stuff.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.350
- [Computer ] We ask
that you cease and desist

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:14.870
all illegal activity.

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:16.190
- [Marcus] They said that
they were something like

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:20.323
a think tank, hobbyists,
volunteer-type of an organization.

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:22.980
- [Computer] There's one last step,

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.463
and we expect you to be honest with us.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.720
- They said we were
ready for the next step

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:31.900
but we had to answer some questions first.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.540
There were questions
about digital liberty,

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:37.720
freedom of information,
some ideological things

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:38.810
that they were working towards

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.293
as one of their organization's goals.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.270
I replied to the email
with all of those answers,

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:47.070
and then I just had to sit and wait.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.293
After a month, I found out
I was a member of Cicada.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:02.650
- [Nox] Marcus and I have been friends

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.773
since before the Cicada years.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:09.230
It was him who convinced
me to come and try it out.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:13.063
He's certainly by far one of
the smartest people I know.

00:11:13.965 --> 00:11:16.433
We met on IRC, actually, Marcus and I.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:20.970
- [Brian] On January 6, 2013,

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.670
Cicada tweeted out a second major puzzle.

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:26.100
It attracted many new would-be solvers,

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.913
one of whom goes by the name Nox Populi.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:33.100
- I'm Nox Populi. I'm 28 years old.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.270
I'm from Alberta; it's a prairie province,

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.853
middle of Canada, middle
of nowhere, really.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:42.560
There's nothing in hours in
every direction from here.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:45.370
It is just empty plains out forever.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.673
And I mean, the city sort of reflects it.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:50.930
I have a full-time job.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:54.230
I do these cryptography
puzzles in my free time.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.340
I have a YouTube series on Cicada.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:01.947
Hello and welcome to part one
of my 2013 Cicada 3301 series.

00:12:01.947 --> 00:12:05.550
The whole point of this
YouTube channel I set up was

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.590
this one big attempt to
explain this to a layperson.

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.760
So if we go to file a new certificate,

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.960
we can make our own identity here.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:17.370
I'm terrible at coming up with
usernames; they're all bad.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:20.040
Nox Populi, I'd actually never intended

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.623
anyone to hear that username.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:26.520
I was listening to a
song called "Vox Populi,"

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.900
which is the Latin term
"voice of the people."

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:34.430
At the time, my girlfriend's
cat was named Nox,

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.860
and he had trapped himself
under a bowl under my desk

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.520
and was bumping into my feet and meowing.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.150
So it was just the two
things I had at the time

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.760
I just threw together and moved on.

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:46.780
To be honest, I'm gonna sleep

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:48.700
with my phone on max volume,
so if it comes at like

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:50.150
four in the morning,
someone just wake me up.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.080
I'll get on here, it doesn't matter.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.683
All right, I'm gonna head, guys.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.110
There's sort of like three
levels of the community.

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:58.910
If you go just up to the general internet,

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:00.690
the number of people interested in Cicada

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:02.853
is ridiculously large.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:06.050
There's things like Facebook Groups.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.300
There's the Wiki.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:09.180
Massive amounts of people.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.040
But most of those people have never

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:12.380
actually worked on Cicada.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.280
They're just kind of interested in it.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.910
Then you kind of take a step closer in,

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:21.640
and there's the IRC communities;

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.530
that's more looking to work on something.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.763
But even that, that's a massive group.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:36.600
When you're actually working on Cicada,

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.270
you pick the few people you know

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:40.400
you're good at working
with, because you're really

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.400
gonna be doing this at insanely fast pace.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.880
And you also need to be sure you can trust

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.290
that if they make a discovery,
they're not just gonna

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:50.863
run off without you, that sort of thing.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.320
You get this step in 2013.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:01.650
Every time you take
another piece of it off,

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:04.150
there's just more and more complexity.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.200
You get this link; it
takes you to this DropBox.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.190
It's just a DropBox links to a file,

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.440
and there's no extension on the file.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.620
It ends up being this disk image.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:16.500
So what you would do is boot from that

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.700
instead of your operating system.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:21.030
It's an operating system on its own,

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.070
so it's sort of like the Cicada OS

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:24.730
that you're loading into on your computer,

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.580
which is actually kind of terrifying.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.890
It's got the whole Linux file structure.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:32.780
Dig around in there, and
that's where the song

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:36.113
first comes from, just sitting
in one of the temp folders.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:41.680
It was called 761.mp3.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.710
If you start looking into
all the different tags on it,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:46.909
it was called "The Instar Emergence."

00:14:46.909 --> 00:14:50.150
(guitar music)

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.660
Kind of classical guitar at first

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.746
and then starts kind of
getting weirder and weirder.

00:14:55.746 --> 00:14:58.410
(guitar music)

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.960
There's this unique
composed piece of music

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:04.783
just for the few people would
ever find it on this disk.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:08.850
The further we went into the puzzle,

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:11.263
the more obscure the clues became.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:17.300
The Mayan numerals,
early 1900s occult stuff.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:22.530
Aleister Crowley, Zen,
Buddhism, Futhark runes.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.640
Every time you move anywhere in Cicada,

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.570
all of the other layers
of it just burst open.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.370
An exceptional amount of
work has gone into making

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:33.760
all of these individual, tiny pieces,

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:35.523
meticulously fit together.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.200
It's like opening the face of
your watch for the first time.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:43.010
You have no concept of
how all of those gears

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:45.920
somehow work into seconds,
minutes, and hours.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:47.670
But suddenly you can see the scope of it,

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:48.693
and it's staggering.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:54.430
Finally, you get to this
website, and it starts a timer

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.693
and starts putting questions up on screen.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.690
A large part of it was multiple choice.

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:06.190
My favorite one is everyone
understands what you mean

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:07.740
when you say it's dark outside.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:13.030
What does it refer to in
the previous sentence?

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.550
They might have an answer, they might not.

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:17.220
It's trying to make you think.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.300
It's sort of like all the zen jokes

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.820
about a sound of one hand clapping.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:25.080
There's not really a good
explanation for what's happening,

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:27.004
but the timer's going down.

00:16:27.004 --> 00:16:29.837
(pulsating music)

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:33.110
- [Computer] Very good.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:35.810
To attain enlightenment, create a gbg key

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:37.253
for your email address.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.250
- So I got the invitation;
I sent back my response.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:46.323
I was so excited, and then I was waiting.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.163
Days turned into weeks, no response.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.113
Nothing ever came.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.470
- [Brian] On January 5th, 2014,

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:01.230
Cicada released its third major puzzle,

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:05.723
a series of steps that led
to a 58-page book of symbols.

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.787
They called it the "Liber Primus."

00:17:09.959 --> 00:17:13.550
- It's 58 pages in a
language you don't speak.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.213
It's insane, staggering.

00:17:17.350 --> 00:17:19.140
We had no starting point.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.600
We don't know if it's steganography.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:22.973
It could've been anything.

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:26.640
'Cause no individual person knows enough

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.370
to even start tackling it alone,

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:30.380
because it's just,
there's too many options.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:31.880
Any one person could work on it

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:35.760
the rest of their lives and
probably not get half way.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:39.060
No one particularly
stood out until Onecool.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:43.130
He was consistently putting
out ideas no one else had

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.320
and work that should have been done

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:46.620
and no one else was doing.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.570
- [Onecool] I'm actually
gonna show you guys something.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:52.120
Look at this.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.990
I believe that this message is a hint.

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.540
Seek and you'll be found.

00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:58.517
- Yeah, okay.
- "Liber Primus" is away.

00:17:58.517 --> 00:18:00.220
I think this is them saying you guys still

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.150
have to solve this before
anything else happen.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:03.810
- I think what you were
getting at, Onecool,

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:06.760
is "Liber Primus" is the
way because of this message.

00:18:12.052 --> 00:18:14.635
(gentle music)

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:24.890
- Onecool, there's not
much of a story behind it,

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:27.757
other than the movie
"Hackers," he was Zero Cool.

00:18:27.757 --> 00:18:30.950
It was the first movie I
ever had on my computer,

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.070
and I watched it, and I loved it.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.780
My brother was like,
"You should be Onecool."

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:37.157
I was like, "All right."

00:18:38.045 --> 00:18:40.933
Been Onecool since mid 2000-something.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:44.370
Growing up in a small town in Nebraska,

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:46.150
it had about a thousand people.

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.280
We lived pretty close to the train tracks,

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:49.850
and then right across the train tracks

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:51.870
is kind of just like nothing.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:56.420
I was in 4-H, and we
would raise these calves,

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.340
feed 'em, brush 'em, walk 'em.

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:00.590
You trained them to like walk circles

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:03.070
and be nice looking animals.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:06.570
Growing up in that town, I
wasn't a popular kid or anything.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.320
That was when I really got into computers

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.390
'cause I was just like,
I don't have any friends.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:13.460
Then I found my computer friends
in middle and high school.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:16.050
My mom wouldn't pay for this game monthly,

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:19.540
so I had to learn how to
host my own server with it

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:22.600
and set it all up, and just
trying to solve these problems,

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.800
so I could have fun with my brothers.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.020
I went to college for computer engineering

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.920
and my master's in mathematics.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:32.933
I work in the field of cryptography.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.130
Cryptography is technically a munition.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:37.920
It's a huge valuable tool in war.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:40.440
So my knowledge of this cryptography,

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.300
it classifies me as a weapon.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.093
So I'm like a walking missile.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:51.220
When I first saw the "Liber Primus,"

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.880
I wasn't sure if we were
gonna be able to solve it.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:57.240
They introduced the difficulty
of not using English.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:01.220
So that itself raised the bar right away

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.980
to be like, well, you can't
just like type this out

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:06.660
on your keyboard, you
have to like transcribe

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.070
each one of these old Nordic runes.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:12.373
I spent many nights transcribing
it letter by letter.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.240
So there's a lot to the "Liber Primus."

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:16.770
Seems like the more time we spent on it,

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.700
the more little oddities we find.

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:22.160
One of the pages was not encrypted.

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:25.060
It was only written in
Runic, so it was a matter

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.900
of substituting a rune
for the English letters.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.350
So that was easy.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:33.450
Another page was encrypted
with a stream of numbers.

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:36.500
So the result of solving
the second-to-last page

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.770
was a message that said we had to seek out

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.940
a page on the deep web.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.440
They said it's everybody's
duty to find this page.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:48.560
The number of possibilities
for what content

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:51.140
might be in the "Liber
Primus," like if all Nebraska

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.280
was buried in six feet of hay
and there's a single needle,

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.923
I've gotta come up with
some way to find that.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.520
Yeah, that's the difficulty.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.920
- So, I did some stuff today

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:07.193
when I could fit it around my other shit.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.208
- Working on puzzles, I've learned a lot,

00:21:11.208 --> 00:21:12.570
I've met a lot of cool people.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:15.190
- I've also been like just
thinking about a lot of shit,

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:18.110
which I kinda wanna like talk
about our process later on.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:19.800
- Yeah, Nox is our Canadian.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.300
He kinda just keeps us going, meeting,

00:21:23.300 --> 00:21:25.520
coming up with ideas, trying them out,

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:26.980
and keeping track of them,

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:28.690
and keeping track of each other.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:30.690
- Onecool, did you have anything to say?

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.747
- I never made it into the
smart program in school.

00:21:35.635 --> 00:21:37.300
- That's hilarious
'cause I'm 100% convinced

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:38.752
you're smarter than me.

00:21:38.752 --> 00:21:41.340
- [Brother Box] (laughs) I'm sorry.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.750
- Brother Box is really
engaged in the community.

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:47.310
He started the Cicada Solvers IRC.

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:49.570
And like he's done a lot of
programming and helped us

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:50.930
with like image analysis.

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.030
- So could you do one?

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.170
- This makes no sense to me.
- Onecool, are you fucking

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:55.984
serious, that's not how you--

00:21:55.984 --> 00:21:57.760
(laughs)

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:01.220
- I love Brother Box,
like I think I understand

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:03.560
his personality, I think
he's a very caring person,

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:06.013
but he also can be like a total asshole.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:11.050
Okay, how about you put the
answer in an encrypted message,

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:13.070
and then I'll have to
break it, how's that?

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.230
- I gotta tell you, right
now our track record

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:17.360
for breaking encrypted
messages isn't so good.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.490
(laughs)

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:21.910
- [Shadow Walker] I'm feeling
so attacked right now.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:23.160
- Shadow Walker, she's really good

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:26.350
at keeping track of things
and teaching people.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.570
Hello, Shadow Walker.
- Hello.

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.140
- Yeah, I think she's great to work with.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:31.380
- [Shadow Walker] I can
actually run our words

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:33.158
with stagnotech.
- Oh, that's useful.

00:22:33.158 --> 00:22:33.991
- That would actually be great.

00:22:33.991 --> 00:22:34.824
- Yeah, that's a good call.

00:22:34.824 --> 00:22:36.020
She lives in the Middle East.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:38.480
I know it's kind of rough
and she is more in danger,

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:41.380
like she has to keep things encrypted

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:42.710
and under the radar.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:44.260
- [Shadow Walker] When you
guys just get this done,

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:45.950
can someone just like send me a PM,

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.516
so I can take a look at it when I wake up?

00:22:48.516 --> 00:22:50.724
- Hi, Marcus.
- Hi, Marcus, are you back?

00:22:50.724 --> 00:22:52.870
- I wrote a little computer program

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:54.570
that can identify characters.

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:55.920
- That is really cool.
- That's awesome.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.880
- [Onecool] Marcus is fun,
great with the crypto.

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:59.230
He doesn't like the runes.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:01.630
He likes the steganography side of things.

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:03.580
- This is all the black dots.

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:05.610
Each of these little clusters up here

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:07.135
is a different kind of rune.

00:23:07.135 --> 00:23:09.040
- You did that?
- Holy shit.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.660
- I think it's a fun way
to spend part of your life,

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:15.370
like working on some hard
problem with people, you know.

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:18.060
- I do believe that we
have the most coordination

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:19.790
of any people that have tried this.

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:22.333
- I would consider this group
like good friends of mine.

00:23:22.333 --> 00:23:24.730
- Okay, well, I think that's a good list

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:25.760
for what we do tomorrow.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:27.477
- Bye, guys, talk to you later.

00:23:27.477 --> 00:23:28.423
- Goodbye.
- Bye.

00:23:31.870 --> 00:23:33.870
- Oh my gosh.
- That's an enigma machine.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.180
- We've really been stuck.
- If you weren't persistent,

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:37.610
you wouldn't be attacking ciphers.

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:38.930
- [Man] We can't drop the problem.

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:40.100
- You wanna see this solved.

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:41.540
I can tell, you got the fire.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.690
- [Man] Governments are
increasingly efficient

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.249
at surveilling their people,

00:23:45.249 --> 00:23:47.220
and this will only get worse in my mind.

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:49.560
- [Woman] I feel like
the flow of information

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.510
in a society should always be free.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:53.180
- The project we ended up working on

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:55.560
was intended to solve a
life-and-death problem

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:58.360
for people who have reason
to fear for their lives.

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.310
- [Man] Someone parked a
van outside of my house.

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:03.910
- So this is the van?
- Jesus Christ, it's real.

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:05.590
- NSA is a big place.

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:09.180
Could one of our folks
conceivably have done it?

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:11.300
That is a very tricky ethical, legal,

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:13.063
moral question to answer.

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.480
- [Man] Cicada believed that
they could sow a little seed

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.607
of curiosity into the minds of people.

00:24:26.059 --> 00:24:28.642
(upbeat music)

